6125312 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125312 #edb/ref 1061 11426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125312 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d81b9ea9-a347-5ebb-91d1-4bc5c4b566e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125312 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125313 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125313 #edb/ref 1061 11429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125313 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f4cc9d43-0b3e-5cd2-8e04-c1f0f43e9e2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125313 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125314 #edb/ref 1061 11432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125314 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f23e88d8-c204-5795-b172-b05000a202c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125314 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125463 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125463 #edb/ref 1061 22675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125463 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c86dfe84-cedf-55cf-bf44-efb12a49ac66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125463 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125741 #edb/ref 1064 "The partial derivative of a function exists where the original function is defined *and* the partial derivative is defined.\n\nFor example, let's consider the function $f(x,y) = \\ln(xy).$ We wish to determine the set of values $(x,y)$ on which the partial derivative $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ exists.\n\nFirst, we need to find the domain of $f$ since we cannot differentiate $f$ at any point that lies outside of its domain. The natural logarithm is defined only for positive inputs, so the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\nD_f = \\{ (x,y)\\, : \\, xy > 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the partial derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ (also denoted $f_x$) is not defined at $x=0.$ So, the domain of $f_x$ is\n\n$$\nD_{f_x} = \\{ (x,y)\\, : \\, x \\ne 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the set where  $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ exists, we take the intersection of the domain of the original function $(D_f)$ with the domain of the partial derivative $(D_{f_x})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ exists on the set $\\{ (x,y)\\, : \\, xy \\gt 0 \\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125741 #edb/ref 1065 2532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125741 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5862db65-6f2d-5256-968d-cb971dd6b2f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125741 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125742 #edb/ref 1064 "From single-variable calculus, if a function $y=f(x)$ is differentiable at a point, then it is continuous at that point.\n\nBut for a multivariable function, even if all partial derivatives are defined at a certain point, it **does not mean** that the function is continuous at that point!\n\nThe reason why partial differentiability does not imply continuity in the case of a multivariable function is that\n\n- the existence of the partial derivatives $f_x$ and $f_y$ depend only on the behavior of $f$ parallel to the $x$- and $y$-axes, whereas\n- the continuity of $f$ depends on its behavior as we approach $(x_0,y_0)$ from any direction.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following multivariable function:\n\n$$f(x,y)= {\\begin{aligned}\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\, & (x,y)\\ne (0,0) \\\\ 0, & (x,y)=(0,0)\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe function $f$ is defined at the point $(0,0)\\mathbin{:}$ $$f(0,0)=0$$\n\nLikewise, the partial derivatives $f_x$ and $f_y$ are both defined at the point $(0,0).$ We can compute them using the limit definition of the partial derivative, as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,0) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[f(0+h,0)-f(0,0)] \\\\ & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[\\frac{(h)(0)}{{h}^{2}+{0}^{2}}-0] \\\\ & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[0-0] \\\\ & =0 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,0) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[f(0,0+h)-f(0,0)] \\\\ & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[\\frac{(0)(h)}{{0}^{2}+{h}^{2}}-0] \\\\ & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[0-0] \\\\ & =0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo the partial derivatives both exist at $(0,0).$ We will now show that the function is not continuous at $(0,0).$\n\nFor the function $f$ to be continuous at $(0,0),$ the limit of the function must be equal to the function value, regardless of the path taken. So, we must have\n\n$$\\lim\\limits_{(x,y) \\to (0,0)} f(x,y) = f(0,0) =0.$$\n\nHowever, this is not the case here. When we evaluate the limit along the path $y=x,$ we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{(x,y)\\to (0,0)\\,\\,y=x}f(x,y) & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{x\\cdot x}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\ & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\ & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, our function $f(x,y)$ is *not* continuous at the point $(0,0),$ even though both of its partial derivatives exist there." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125742 #edb/ref 1065 2533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125742 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ca47070b-a1d3-53ad-8f57-2dbd4e770dce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125742 #edb/ref 1067 "Partial Differentiability Does Not Imply Continuity" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333981 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333981 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333981 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6d023b1a-e54c-5a09-986e-c64e8ea0ce13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334588 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334588 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334588 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b6aad436-bdd3-559d-8993-f9ec2e12126f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334661 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334661 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334661 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c0e883eb-0fef-5f4c-9a6c-403643063014" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334828 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334828 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334828 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d284e0b7-af0b-5d1a-99e5-eded90f88db0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335153 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335153 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335153 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f6908825-368b-5836-af84-e8c9e08903f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335603 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335603 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335603 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335603 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335603 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186335153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335603 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335603 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335603 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186334828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335603 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c726d8d6-8230-5663-b6a4-988732e72c43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335822 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335822 #edb/ref 1069 "q-65255" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335822 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335822 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=xy{e}^{x/y}?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(0,1)$is defined\n- ${f}_{y}(1,0)$ is defined\n- Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,1)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335822 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b22bd39f-49d5-56c7-8797-705e83876e55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335822 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy{e}^{x/y}) \\\\  & =y{e}^{x/y}+xy{e}^{x/y}(\\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =y{e}^{x/y}+x{e}^{x/y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy{e}^{x/y}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{x/y}+xy{e}^{x/y}(-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{x/y}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}{e}^{x/y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(0,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,1)=0\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{0/1}=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1)=1\\cdot {e}^{0/1}+0\\cdot {e}^{0/1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0)$ into ${f}_{y}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{y}(1,0)=1\\cdot {e}^{1/0}-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{0}\\cdot {e}^{1/0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,1)=1\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}=e,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}+1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}=2e \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,1) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{1}\\cdot {e}^{1/1}=e-e=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337292 #edb/ref 1080 "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337292 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337292 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8b4579a6-884f-5620-9c48-03f859207fbd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337566 #edb/ref 1080 "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337566 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337566 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "aa211335-2ec0-5533-aaf0-0747d0111e9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337575 #edb/ref 1080 "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<0}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337575 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337575 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ab12ccaa-59a3-5391-8885-0e57cfa9220d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337875 #edb/ref 1080 "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337875 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337875 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d30b5af1-be96-52a2-983e-53d94d787930" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337921 #edb/ref 1080 "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337921 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337921 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d932ef34-432a-58eb-96dc-98456b7b3465" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338315 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186337292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338315 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186337566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338315 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186337575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338315 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186337875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338315 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186337921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338315 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338315 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338315 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186337292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338315 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "167ce942-9903-5739-b46e-634adbd1db2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339043 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339043 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72005" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339043 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339043 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $g(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $g$ exist." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339043 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "329fb25a-c39e-587a-8c51-720f867dc9e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339043 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $g(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${g}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409538 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186409560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409538 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186409561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409538 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186409562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409538 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186409563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409538 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186409564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409538 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409538 #edb/ref 1045 "Partial Differentiability of Multivariable Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409538 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186409562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409538 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409538 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409